window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2022-12-2", "source": "2022年12月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: In this task, you are to write an essay on the importance o f developing a healthy lifestyle among college students. You will have 30 minutes for the task. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "A healthy lifestyle plays a vital role in the life of every college student. As campus life grows busier with\nassignments and screens, I firmly believe that keeping fit, eating well and sleeping regularly is not a trivial\nhabit but the very foundation of a successful university experience.\nFor one thing, a sound body directly boosts learning efficiency. A student who stays up all night rushing a due\ndate and lives on takeout will struggle to concentrate the next morning, while regular exercise and balanced\nmeals keep the mind sharp in class. For another, a healthy routine stabilizes our emotions. Sitting still for\nhours breeds anxiety, whereas a short run often washes away the pressure of exams and eases loneliness far\nfrom home.\nIn conclusion, a healthy lifestyle is not optional but essential for college students. The sooner we build it, the\nmore energy and confidence we will carry into our studies and future lives."}, "translation": {"prompt": "立春 (Start of Spring )在中国农历中表示春天的开始。立春之后， 白天变得更长，天气也愈发温暖，万物开始复苏，大地充满生机。人们常说\"一年之计在于春\"，农民在这个时节开始播种，为全年的丰收打下基础。中国人早在三千年前就己开始在立春这一天举行庆祝活动。数百年来，迎春一直是民间的重要习俗。在春暖花开的日子里，人们常常外出游玩，欣赏春天的美景。", "sample": "Lichun (Start of Spring) marks the beginning of spring in the Chinese lunar calendar. After Lichun, the days grow longer and the weather becomes warmer; everything comes back to life, and the earth is full of vitality. As people often say, \"The whole year's work depends on a good start in spring.\" At this time of year, farmers start sowing to lay the foundation for a good harvest throughout the whole year. In fact, the Chinese have started to hold celebrations on this day as early as 3,000 years ago. For centuries, welcoming spring has been an important folk custom. Today, on warm spring days when flowers are in full bloom, people often go out for outings to enjoy the beautiful scenery of spring."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2022-12-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Parenting brings fathers more joy than it does mothers, according to a new study. The research examined three studies  __26__  more than 18,000 participants. Across all three, parenthood was  __27__  with more positive wellbeing outcomes for dads than for mums.\nSo why are fathers happier than mothers? \"Fathers may fare better than mothers in part due to how they spend time with their children,\" said lead author Katherine Nelson-Coffey. In one study, the authors  __28__  that dads were more likely to take \"playing\" as an  __29__  activity both when caring for their kids and spending time with their kids. \"Playing with their children likely offers parents opportunities to experience positive feelings and  __30__  closeness with their children,\" they say.\nFathers also did better than men without kids, reporting greater happiness, life satisfaction, and fewer  __31__  symptoms. They also reported greater connectedness and autonomy(自主). For mums,  __32__  , compared to women without children, the results weren't quite as positive. Mums reported greater autonomy, but also \"greater trouble\" and fewer positive  __33__  .\nMums reported happier moods while interacting with their kids, compared to other experiences, but not while engaging  __34__  in childcare. \"This difference suggests that how mothers and fathers spend time with their children might have important  __35__  for their wellbeing,\" the authors write. They suspect that mums may be less happy than dads because they're more likely to have higher expectations about parenthood. As such, they're more likely to be \"let down\" by the experience.", "matchIntro": "Learning to say no", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Not doing something will always be faster than doing it. This philosophy applies in many areas of life. For example, there is no meeting that goes faster than not having a meeting at all. This is not to say you should never attend another meeting, but the truth is that we say \"yes\" to too many things we don't actually want to do."}, {"label": "B", "text": "How often do people ask you to do something and you just reply, \"Sure.\" Three days later, you're overwhelmed by how much is on your to-do list. We become frustrated by our obligations even though we were the ones who said \"yes\" to them in the first place. Even worse, people will occasionally fight to do things that waste time. You don't have to do something just because it exists. It's worth asking if things are necessary. Many of them are not, and a simple \"no\" will be more productive than whatever work the most efficient person can cope with. But if the benefits of saying \"no\" are so obvious, then why do we say \"yes\" so often?"}, {"label": "C", "text": "We say \"yes\" to many requests not because we want to do them, but because we don't want to be seen as rude or unhelpful. Often, we have to consider saying \"no\" to someone we will interact with again in the future—our co-worker, our spouse, our family and friends. Saying \"no\" to our superiors at work can be particularly difficult. In these situations, I like the approach recommended in Essentialism by Greg McKeown. He writes, \"Remind your superiors what you would be neglecting if you said 'yes' and force them to deal with the trade-off. For example, if your manager comes to you and asks you to do X, you can respond with 'Yes, I'm happy to make this the priority. Which of these other projects should I deprioritize to pay attention to this new project?'\""}, {"label": "D", "text": "Collaborating with others is an important element of life. The thought of straining the relationship outweighs the commitment of our time and energy. For this reason, it can be helpful to be gracious in your response. Do whatever favors you can, and be warm-hearted and direct when you have to say no. But even after we have accounted for these social considerations, many of us still seem to do a poor job of managing the trade-off between yes and no. We find ourselves over-committed to things that don't meaningfully improve or support those around us, and certainly don't improve our own lives."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Perhaps one issue is how we think about the meaning of yes and no. The words \"yes\" and \"no\" get so often used in comparison with each other that it feels like they carry equal weight in conversation. In reality, they're not just opposite in meaning, but of entirely different magnitudes in commitment. When you say \"no\", you're only saying \"no\" to one option. When you say \"yes\", you're saying \"no\" to every other option. I like how economist Tim Harford put it, \"Every time we say 'yes' to a request, we're also saying 'no' to anything else we might accomplish with the time?\" Once you're committed to something, you've already decided how that future block of time will be spent. In other words, saying \"no\" saves you time in the future. Saying \"yes\" costs you time in the future. \"No\" is a form of time credit. You retain the ability to spend your future time however you want. \"Yes\" is a form of time debt. You have to pay back your commitment at some point."}, {"label": "F", "text": "\"No\" is a decision. \"Yes\" is a responsibility. Saying \"no\" is sometimes seen as a luxury that only those in power can afford. And it's true: turning down opportunities is easier when you can fall back on the safety net provided by power, money, and authority. But it's also true that saying \"no\" is not merely a privilege reserved for the successful. It's also a strategy that can help you become successful. Saying \"no\" is an important skill to develop at any stage of your career because it retains the most important asset in life: your time. As investor Pedro Sorrentino put it, \"If you don't guard your time, people will steal it from you.\" You need to say \"no\" to whatever isn't leading you toward your goals."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Nobody embodied this idea better than Steve Jobs, who said, \"People think focus means saying 'yes' to the thing you've got to focus on. But that's not what it means at all. It means saying 'no' to the hundred other good ideas that there are. You have to pick carefully.\" Jobs had another great quote about saying \"no\": \"I'm actually as proud of the things we haven't done as the things I have done. Innovation is saying 'no' to 1,000 things.\""}, {"label": "H", "text": "Over time, as you continue to improve and succeed, your strategy needs to change. The opportunity cost of your time increases as you become more successful. At first, you just eliminate the obvious distractions and explore the rest. As your skills improve and you learn to separate what works from what doesn't, you have to continually increase your threshold for saying \"yes\". You still need to say \"no\" to distractions, but you also need to learn to say \"no\" to opportunities that were previously good uses of time, so you can make space for better uses of time. It's a good problem to have, but it can be a tough skill to master."}, {"label": "I", "text": "What is true about health is also true about productivity: an ounce of prevention is worth a pound of cure. More effort is wasted doing things that don't matter than is wasted doing things inefficiently. And if that is the case, elimination is a more useful skill than optimization. I'm reminded of the famous Peter Drucker quote, \"There is nothing so useless as doing efficiently that which should not be done at all.\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "We're eating more fish than ever these days. At around 20 kilograms per person global fish consumption is now more than twice what it was in the 1960s. What's really remarkable, though, is where that fish comes from.\nFor the first time in human history, most of our aquatic(水产的) food now comes from farming rather than fishing.\nPeople ate around 73 million tonnes of farmed fish—just more than half of the volume of fish that humans consumed—in 2014. That's out of a total fish supply of 167 million tonnes; the remaining 20 million or so tonnes go into things like animal feed and medical products.\nTo keep eating fish at the current rate, we're definitely going to need to keep aquaculture(水产养殖) developing. That's because the volume of fish caught in the wild has leveled off since the 1990s.\nBack in 1974, only 10% of marine fish stocks had been overfished. Now, more than three-tenths are. Only a tenth of our oceans' fish stocks could sustain heavier fishing than current levels.\nBut while catchings at sea have suffered, fish-farming has been growing at a fast rate. A lot of that is coming from China, which produces 60% of the world's farmed fish. In fact, some 35 countries, including China, now produce more farmed than wild-caught fish.\nThis shift toward aquaculture isn't just good for ensuring salmon(三文鱼) on your plate; it's also crucial to ensuring food security and sustainability. By 2050, the world will need to feed an estimated 9.7 billion people. They'll have to get their protein somewhere. However, raising cattle, pigs, and other land-based animals requires vast sums of grain and water. For example, pound for pound, beef requires 15 times more feed to raise than carp, a freshwater fish farmed all over Asia. That grain—and the water needed to grow it—could be consumed by people instead.\nHowever, aquaculture is no silver bullet. In some southeast Asian countries, shrimp farming does disastrous damage to marine ecosystems. Despite these problems, however, shrimp continues to be among the most popular sea foods worldwide.", "47": "We're eating more fish than ever these days. At around 20 kilograms per person global fish consumption is now more than twice what it was in the 1960s. What's really remarkable, though, is where that fish comes from.\nFor the first time in human history, most of our aquatic(水产的) food now comes from farming rather than fishing.\nPeople ate around 73 million tonnes of farmed fish—just more than half of the volume of fish that humans consumed—in 2014. That's out of a total fish supply of 167 million tonnes; the remaining 20 million or so tonnes go into things like animal feed and medical products.\nTo keep eating fish at the current rate, we're definitely going to need to keep aquaculture(水产养殖) developing. That's because the volume of fish caught in the wild has leveled off since the 1990s.\nBack in 1974, only 10% of marine fish stocks had been overfished. Now, more than three-tenths are. Only a tenth of our oceans' fish stocks could sustain heavier fishing than current levels.\nBut while catchings at sea have suffered, fish-farming has been growing at a fast rate. A lot of that is coming from China, which produces 60% of the world's farmed fish. In fact, some 35 countries, including China, now produce more farmed than wild-caught fish.\nThis shift toward aquaculture isn't just good for ensuring salmon(三文鱼) on your plate; it's also crucial to ensuring food security and sustainability. By 2050, the world will need to feed an estimated 9.7 billion people. They'll have to get their protein somewhere. However, raising cattle, pigs, and other land-based animals requires vast sums of grain and water. For example, pound for pound, beef requires 15 times more feed to raise than carp, a freshwater fish farmed all over Asia. That grain—and the water needed to grow it—could be consumed by people instead.\nHowever, aquaculture is no silver bullet. In some southeast Asian countries, shrimp farming does disastrous damage to marine ecosystems. Despite these problems, however, shrimp continues to be among the most popular sea foods worldwide.", "48": "We're eating more fish than ever these days. At around 20 kilograms per person global fish consumption is now more than twice what it was in the 1960s. What's really remarkable, though, is where that fish comes from.\nFor the first time in human history, most of our aquatic(水产的) food now comes from farming rather than fishing.\nPeople ate around 73 million tonnes of farmed fish—just more than half of the volume of fish that humans consumed—in 2014. That's out of a total fish supply of 167 million tonnes; the remaining 20 million or so tonnes go into things like animal feed and medical products.\nTo keep eating fish at the current rate, we're definitely going to need to keep aquaculture(水产养殖) developing. That's because the volume of fish caught in the wild has leveled off since the 1990s.\nBack in 1974, only 10% of marine fish stocks had been overfished. Now, more than three-tenths are. Only a tenth of our oceans' fish stocks could sustain heavier fishing than current levels.\nBut while catchings at sea have suffered, fish-farming has been growing at a fast rate. A lot of that is coming from China, which produces 60% of the world's farmed fish. In fact, some 35 countries, including China, now produce more farmed than wild-caught fish.\nThis shift toward aquaculture isn't just good for ensuring salmon(三文鱼) on your plate; it's also crucial to ensuring food security and sustainability. By 2050, the world will need to feed an estimated 9.7 billion people. They'll have to get their protein somewhere. However, raising cattle, pigs, and other land-based animals requires vast sums of grain and water. For example, pound for pound, beef requires 15 times more feed to raise than carp, a freshwater fish farmed all over Asia. That grain—and the water needed to grow it—could be consumed by people instead.\nHowever, aquaculture is no silver bullet. In some southeast Asian countries, shrimp farming does disastrous damage to marine ecosystems. Despite these problems, however, shrimp continues to be among the most popular sea foods worldwide.", "49": "We're eating more fish than ever these days. At around 20 kilograms per person global fish consumption is now more than twice what it was in the 1960s. What's really remarkable, though, is where that fish comes from.\nFor the first time in human history, most of our aquatic(水产的) food now comes from farming rather than fishing.\nPeople ate around 73 million tonnes of farmed fish—just more than half of the volume of fish that humans consumed—in 2014. That's out of a total fish supply of 167 million tonnes; the remaining 20 million or so tonnes go into things like animal feed and medical products.\nTo keep eating fish at the current rate, we're definitely going to need to keep aquaculture(水产养殖) developing. That's because the volume of fish caught in the wild has leveled off since the 1990s.\nBack in 1974, only 10% of marine fish stocks had been overfished. Now, more than three-tenths are. Only a tenth of our oceans' fish stocks could sustain heavier fishing than current levels.\nBut while catchings at sea have suffered, fish-farming has been growing at a fast rate. A lot of that is coming from China, which produces 60% of the world's farmed fish. In fact, some 35 countries, including China, now produce more farmed than wild-caught fish.\nThis shift toward aquaculture isn't just good for ensuring salmon(三文鱼) on your plate; it's also crucial to ensuring food security and sustainability. By 2050, the world will need to feed an estimated 9.7 billion people. They'll have to get their protein somewhere. However, raising cattle, pigs, and other land-based animals requires vast sums of grain and water. For example, pound for pound, beef requires 15 times more feed to raise than carp, a freshwater fish farmed all over Asia. That grain—and the water needed to grow it—could be consumed by people instead.\nHowever, aquaculture is no silver bullet. In some southeast Asian countries, shrimp farming does disastrous damage to marine ecosystems. Despite these problems, however, shrimp continues to be among the most popular sea foods worldwide.", "50": "We're eating more fish than ever these days. At around 20 kilograms per person global fish consumption is now more than twice what it was in the 1960s. What's really remarkable, though, is where that fish comes from.\nFor the first time in human history, most of our aquatic(水产的) food now comes from farming rather than fishing.\nPeople ate around 73 million tonnes of farmed fish—just more than half of the volume of fish that humans consumed—in 2014. That's out of a total fish supply of 167 million tonnes; the remaining 20 million or so tonnes go into things like animal feed and medical products.\nTo keep eating fish at the current rate, we're definitely going to need to keep aquaculture(水产养殖) developing. That's because the volume of fish caught in the wild has leveled off since the 1990s.\nBack in 1974, only 10% of marine fish stocks had been overfished. Now, more than three-tenths are. Only a tenth of our oceans' fish stocks could sustain heavier fishing than current levels.\nBut while catchings at sea have suffered, fish-farming has been growing at a fast rate. A lot of that is coming from China, which produces 60% of the world's farmed fish. In fact, some 35 countries, including China, now produce more farmed than wild-caught fish.\nThis shift toward aquaculture isn't just good for ensuring salmon(三文鱼) on your plate; it's also crucial to ensuring food security and sustainability. By 2050, the world will need to feed an estimated 9.7 billion people. They'll have to get their protein somewhere. However, raising cattle, pigs, and other land-based animals requires vast sums of grain and water. For example, pound for pound, beef requires 15 times more feed to raise than carp, a freshwater fish farmed all over Asia. That grain—and the water needed to grow it—could be consumed by people instead.\nHowever, aquaculture is no silver bullet. In some southeast Asian countries, shrimp farming does disastrous damage to marine ecosystems. Despite these problems, however, shrimp continues to be among the most popular sea foods worldwide.", "51": "In 2020, the Nobel Peace Prize was awarded to the World Food Programme (WFP).\nWhy a Nobel Prize for the WFP, and why now? In 2019, the WFP assisted nearly 100 million people in 88 countries. It is the safety net for those who fall off the edge of existence. It is a response to solving the problem of food instability. Its Nobel Prize reminds us all of the moral hazard in imagining that the poor and vulnerable are somebody else's problem.\nThe WFP has been around since 1961 and has been the global coordinator of nationally based efforts to avoid disasters with food aid. Despite decades of effort to eliminate hunger, the latest estimate is that about 11% of people on the planet (about 820 million people) are suffering daily undernourishment. Progress at reducing undernourishment has stopped despite gains through the 1990s and 2000s.\nDeveloped countries sometimes offer food and aid to developing ones, but at a price. One American philosopher stated that addressing the needs of the poor and vulnerable is about more than money—it is mostly about creating conditions under which prosperity and opportunity can thrive. When aid is offered with heavy conditions attached, like loan repayment or food for resources, it often widens the gap between rich and poor and sustains the old world order. This is why the work of the WFP is so vital.\nThe scientific community, however, can provide a helping hand to the WFP. By sharing knowledge of agriculture and climate with peers in countries most vulnerable to poverty and hunger, scientists can help reduce these problems. By making its voice heard, science can lead by example. The ability to overcome food shortages that must be built into some of the poorest countries will not come from loans from wealthy countries, which may have food problems of their own, or world economic institutions. This ability will be built upon self-confident people using open and shared scientific knowledge to pull themselves out of their misery.", "52": "In 2020, the Nobel Peace Prize was awarded to the World Food Programme (WFP).\nWhy a Nobel Prize for the WFP, and why now? In 2019, the WFP assisted nearly 100 million people in 88 countries. It is the safety net for those who fall off the edge of existence. It is a response to solving the problem of food instability. Its Nobel Prize reminds us all of the moral hazard in imagining that the poor and vulnerable are somebody else's problem.\nThe WFP has been around since 1961 and has been the global coordinator of nationally based efforts to avoid disasters with food aid. Despite decades of effort to eliminate hunger, the latest estimate is that about 11% of people on the planet (about 820 million people) are suffering daily undernourishment. Progress at reducing undernourishment has stopped despite gains through the 1990s and 2000s.\nDeveloped countries sometimes offer food and aid to developing ones, but at a price. One American philosopher stated that addressing the needs of the poor and vulnerable is about more than money—it is mostly about creating conditions under which prosperity and opportunity can thrive. When aid is offered with heavy conditions attached, like loan repayment or food for resources, it often widens the gap between rich and poor and sustains the old world order. This is why the work of the WFP is so vital.\nThe scientific community, however, can provide a helping hand to the WFP. By sharing knowledge of agriculture and climate with peers in countries most vulnerable to poverty and hunger, scientists can help reduce these problems. By making its voice heard, science can lead by example. The ability to overcome food shortages that must be built into some of the poorest countries will not come from loans from wealthy countries, which may have food problems of their own, or world economic institutions. This ability will be built upon self-confident people using open and shared scientific knowledge to pull themselves out of their misery.", "53": "In 2020, the Nobel Peace Prize was awarded to the World Food Programme (WFP).\nWhy a Nobel Prize for the WFP, and why now? In 2019, the WFP assisted nearly 100 million people in 88 countries. It is the safety net for those who fall off the edge of existence. It is a response to solving the problem of food instability. Its Nobel Prize reminds us all of the moral hazard in imagining that the poor and vulnerable are somebody else's problem.\nThe WFP has been around since 1961 and has been the global coordinator of nationally based efforts to avoid disasters with food aid. Despite decades of effort to eliminate hunger, the latest estimate is that about 11% of people on the planet (about 820 million people) are suffering daily undernourishment. Progress at reducing undernourishment has stopped despite gains through the 1990s and 2000s.\nDeveloped countries sometimes offer food and aid to developing ones, but at a price. One American philosopher stated that addressing the needs of the poor and vulnerable is about more than money—it is mostly about creating conditions under which prosperity and opportunity can thrive. When aid is offered with heavy conditions attached, like loan repayment or food for resources, it often widens the gap between rich and poor and sustains the old world order. This is why the work of the WFP is so vital.\nThe scientific community, however, can provide a helping hand to the WFP. By sharing knowledge of agriculture and climate with peers in countries most vulnerable to poverty and hunger, scientists can help reduce these problems. By making its voice heard, science can lead by example. The ability to overcome food shortages that must be built into some of the poorest countries will not come from loans from wealthy countries, which may have food problems of their own, or world economic institutions. This ability will be built upon self-confident people using open and shared scientific knowledge to pull themselves out of their misery.", "54": "In 2020, the Nobel Peace Prize was awarded to the World Food Programme (WFP).\nWhy a Nobel Prize for the WFP, and why now? In 2019, the WFP assisted nearly 100 million people in 88 countries. It is the safety net for those who fall off the edge of existence. It is a response to solving the problem of food instability. Its Nobel Prize reminds us all of the moral hazard in imagining that the poor and vulnerable are somebody else's problem.\nThe WFP has been around since 1961 and has been the global coordinator of nationally based efforts to avoid disasters with food aid. Despite decades of effort to eliminate hunger, the latest estimate is that about 11% of people on the planet (about 820 million people) are suffering daily undernourishment. Progress at reducing undernourishment has stopped despite gains through the 1990s and 2000s.\nDeveloped countries sometimes offer food and aid to developing ones, but at a price. One American philosopher stated that addressing the needs of the poor and vulnerable is about more than money—it is mostly about creating conditions under which prosperity and opportunity can thrive. When aid is offered with heavy conditions attached, like loan repayment or food for resources, it often widens the gap between rich and poor and sustains the old world order. This is why the work of the WFP is so vital.\nThe scientific community, however, can provide a helping hand to the WFP. By sharing knowledge of agriculture and climate with peers in countries most vulnerable to poverty and hunger, scientists can help reduce these problems. By making its voice heard, science can lead by example. The ability to overcome food shortages that must be built into some of the poorest countries will not come from loans from wealthy countries, which may have food problems of their own, or world economic institutions. This ability will be built upon self-confident people using open and shared scientific knowledge to pull themselves out of their misery.", "55": "In 2020, the Nobel Peace Prize was awarded to the World Food Programme (WFP).\nWhy a Nobel Prize for the WFP, and why now? In 2019, the WFP assisted nearly 100 million people in 88 countries. It is the safety net for those who fall off the edge of existence. It is a response to solving the problem of food instability. Its Nobel Prize reminds us all of the moral hazard in imagining that the poor and vulnerable are somebody else's problem.\nThe WFP has been around since 1961 and has been the global coordinator of nationally based efforts to avoid disasters with food aid. Despite decades of effort to eliminate hunger, the latest estimate is that about 11% of people on the planet (about 820 million people) are suffering daily undernourishment. Progress at reducing undernourishment has stopped despite gains through the 1990s and 2000s.\nDeveloped countries sometimes offer food and aid to developing ones, but at a price. One American philosopher stated that addressing the needs of the poor and vulnerable is about more than money—it is mostly about creating conditions under which prosperity and opportunity can thrive. When aid is offered with heavy conditions attached, like loan repayment or food for resources, it often widens the gap between rich and poor and sustains the old world order. This is why the work of the WFP is so vital.\nThe scientific community, however, can provide a helping hand to the WFP. By sharing knowledge of agriculture and climate with peers in countries most vulnerable to poverty and hunger, scientists can help reduce these problems. By making its voice heard, science can lead by example. The ability to overcome food shortages that must be built into some of the poorest countries will not come from loans from wealthy countries, which may have food problems of their own, or world economic institutions. This ability will be built upon self-confident people using open and shared scientific knowledge to pull themselves out of their misery."}, "questions": [{"id": "2022-12-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What event will take place in Glasgow?", "options": ["A) A free car show.", "B) A yearly concert.", "C) A sports competition.", "D) A pipe band contest."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） A pipe band contest.\n【定位】The World Pipe Band Championships will take place in Glasgow on Saturday.\n「世界风笛乐队锦标赛」将于周六在格拉斯哥举行。\n【信号】开篇点题句。新闻首句 The World Pipe Band Championships will take place in\nGlasgow on Saturday. 与题干 What event will take place in Glasgow? 在结构上几乎一\n一对应(will take place in Glasgow 完全相同)，这就是新闻「导语 = 答案」的强信号。\n【替换】The World Pipe Band Championships ↔ A pipe band contest\nchampionships 同义替换为 contest\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音通篇没出现任何 car show、汽车相关表述，A free car show. 属凭空想象。它唯一沾\n边的是 free 这个词——录音里 The event is free. 确实说了 free，于是把「免费」错按到\n「车展」上，是典型的把原词嫁接到错对象。\n借用原文：The event is free.\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音说的是 the annual event，annual 与选项里的 yearly 同义，但核心活动是 pipe\nband championships，不是 concert(音乐会)。yearly 这个原义被偷出来盖到一个录音从\n未提到的 concert 上。\n借用原文：the annual event\nC) 偷换概念\nA sports competition. 中 competition 与 D 项的 contest 几乎同义——录音里的\nChampionships 翻译成 competition 也讲得通，但风笛比赛属于音乐文化赛事，不是体育\n(sports)赛事。把 sports 这一定语换成 pipe band 才对。\n借用原文：Over 120 of the world's best pipe bands will compete at the championships.", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What does Glasgow City Council say the event will do?", "options": ["A) Improve the image of Glasgow city.", "B) Enrich the local culture of Glasgow.", "C) Contribute a lot to the local economy.", "D) Entertain people in local communities."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Contribute a lot to the local economy.\n【定位】Glasgow City Council says it is estimated to bring £300,000 to the local economy.\n格拉斯哥市议会表示，预计本次活动将为当地经济带来 30 万英镑的收入。\n【信号】题干主语 + 金额词同时命中。原文 Glasgow City Council says it is estimated to bring\n£300,000 to the local economy. 与题干 What does Glasgow City Council say the\nevent will do? 在「Glasgow City Council says」上几乎逐字对接，再叠加金额\n£300,000，是双重答案信号。\n【替换】bring £300,000 to the local economy ↔ Contribute a lot to the local economy.\nbring 金额 = contribute a lot\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音通篇无 image / reputation 等谈论城市形象的字眼。它和 C 在「为城市好」的方向上\n同方向，容易让考生「感觉对」，但题目问的是 Council 实际说了什么，没说就是没说。\nB) 张冠李戴\n录音里 The first World Pipe Band Championships were held in Edinburgh in 1947. 等\n句确实交代了赛事历史，乍听有文化色彩，但「丰富格拉斯哥本地文化」 (Enrich the local\nculture of Glasgow.) 这一定性并不是 Glasgow City Council 给的，更不是题干所问。\n借用原文：It has been held regularly in Glasgow since 1986.\nD) 过度推断\n录音里 Over 4,000 pipers and drummers are expected to descend on the city for the\nannual event. 说的是有 4000 名风笛手鼓手前来参赛，并未提及「娱乐当地社区居民」。\n选项把「热闹场面」这一表象误读成「让 local communities 开心」，方向偏。\n借用原文：Over 4,000 pipers and drummers are expected to descend on the city for the\nannual event.\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 Summer doesn't begin for two more days, but scientists are already sounding the alarm about\ndangerous ice melts going on right now in Greenland.\n距离夏季开始还有两天,但科学家们已经对格陵兰岛目前正在发生的危险冰层融化发出警报。\n2 The vast island usually locks away enough fresh water in its ice sheet, which if melted can raise\nglobal sea levels by six meters. And research shows that in recent years its ice has melted faster than\never before. This month, as temperatures in northwestern Greenland approach all-time highs, researchers\nare encountering unusual, surprising levels of melting ice in the region.\n这座广袤的岛屿通常将足够多的淡水锁在其冰盖中,如果融化可使全球海平面上升六米。研究表明,近年来\n其冰层融化速度比以往任何时候都快。本月,随着格陵兰岛西北部气温接近历史最高值,研究人员在该地区\n遇到了异常且令人惊讶的冰层融化水平。\n3 The most significant concern here is how early this is all happening. Greenland experiences annual ice\nmelt and growth cycles. But this year, the melting season began in early May. That's a month earlier\nthan normal and something that's happened only once before in the historical record in 2016.\n这里最重大的担忧是这一切发生得如此之早。格陵兰岛经历着年度冰层融化和增长周期。但今年,融化季节\n始于五月初。这比正常情况提前了一个月,而且在历史记录中只在2016年发生过一次。\n4 That means that not only is the ice melting very fast, it has more time to melt this summer than it\nwould in a normal climate system. Longer melt seasons like this one seem to lead to much greater\ncontributions to sea-level rise than in previous decades.\n这意味着冰层不仅融化得非常快,而且今年夏天它有比正常气候系统中更多的时间融化。像这样更长的融化\n季节似乎会导致对海平面上升的贡献远大于前几十年。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What is scientists' warning in the news report?", "options": ["A) Surprising rise in global sea levels.", "B) Dangerous ice melts in Greenland.", "C) Changing weather patterns in summer.", "D) Record growth of Greenland's ice sheets."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （主旨题） Dangerous ice melts in Greenland.\n【定位】Summer doesn't begin for two more days, but scientists are already sounding the alarm about\ndangerous ice melts going on right now in Greenland.\n夏天还有两天才开始，但科学家们已经在为格陵兰岛正在发生的危险融冰拉响警报。\n【信号】导语 + 转折 but + sounding the alarm。答案信号有三层叠加：第一，这是新闻第一句\n（导语，新闻惯例第一句点题）；第二，句中 but 是强转折，新闻里 but 之后才是真正要\n说的事；第三，紧跟 sounding the alarm（拉响警报）正是题干 warning 的同义信号，三\n者合在一起把答案句钉死在这一句。\n【替换】sounding the alarm ↔ scientists' warning\n拉响警报等于发出警告\ndangerous ice melts going on right now in Greenland ↔ Dangerous ice melts in Greenland.\n几乎是原句压缩\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 借用原文第 2 段 raise global sea levels by six meters 里的 sea levels 做诱饵，但原文\n那句只是『如果冰盖融化，可能抬升海平面 6 米』，是一个假设性的后果，且 surprising\nrise in global sea levels 并未在录音中实际宣布，把『潜在后果』当成『已经发生的警\n告』，属偷换概念。\n借用原文：which if melted can raise global sea levels by six meters\nC) 未提及\nC 盗用原文里 Summer 与第 3、4 段反复出现的『季节、夏季』语境，把话题悄悄从『冰\n融』扩大到『夏季天气模式的变化』。整则新闻没有讨论 weather patterns（如降雨、风\n暴等气象格局），范围被无故放大。\n借用原文：Summer doesn't begin for two more days\nD) 与原文相反\nD 把录音里 ice melts、ice sheet 这些原词搬过来，但把动作整个反过来说成 record\ngrowth of Greenland's ice sheets（『创纪录地增长』），与原文 its ice has melted\nfaster than ever before 方向相反，是典型的『反向』诱饵。\n借用原文：its ice has melted faster than ever before", "points": ["听力·长对话", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What do we learn about this year's ice melting?", "options": ["A) It began in late May.", "B) It lasted three months.", "C) It started a month earlier than usual.", "D) It ended a month earlier than before."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It started a month earlier than usual.\n【定位】But this year, the melting season began in early May. That's a month earlier than normal and\nsomething that's happened only once before in the historical record in 2016.\n但今年，融冰季在 5 月初就开始了。这比往年提前了一个月，是历史上仅在 2016 年出现过\n一次的情况。\n【信号】转折 But this year 加时间反常。信号有两层：第一，But this year 是强转折，前面在讲\n『通常的融冰周期』，But 一拐就来到『今年反常』的落点；第二，紧跟出现的 a month\nearlier than normal 与题干 this year 严丝合缝，正是答案承载句。\n【替换】the melting season began in early May ↔ It started a month earlier than usual.\nbegan 对应 started\na month earlier than normal ↔ a month earlier than usual\nthan normal 等于 than usual\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 借用原文 began in early May 里的 May 做诱饵，但把 early May（5 月初）偷换成 late\nMay（5 月底）；只要听清 early 与 late 这一字之差，就能识破。\n借用原文：the melting season began in early May\nB) 未提及\nB 项 It lasted three months. 在录音中完全没有出现『三个月』或任何与 three months 对\n应的时长表述，是利用考生听到 Longer melt seasons 就想去补一个具体时长的心理凭空\n捏造的数字。\nD) 与原文相反\nD 与正确项 C 只差一个动词：started 变 ended。原文清清楚楚说的是融冰季『开始』提前\n一个月（began in early May），而非『结束』提前一个月；D 把动作整个反向，是典型的\n『开始或结束』互换陷阱。\n借用原文：the melting season began in early May", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What happened on the streets of Blackhall Colliery?", "options": ["A) Bundles of £20 notes kept turning up.", "B) A villager was searching for his lost cash.", "C) Local policemen came across bundles of £20 notes.", "D) A bundle containing thousands of pounds got stolen."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Bundles of £20 notes kept turning up.\n【定位】Bundles containing thousands of pounds in cash keep turning up on the streets of Blackhall\nColliery in northern England, according to police.\n据警方称，装有数千英镑现金的成捆钞票不断出现在英格兰北部布莱克霍尔矿区的街道上。\n【信号】新闻导语首句即落点。答案信号是\"导语首句\"：Bundles containing thousands of\npounds in cash keep turning up on the streets 正是全篇第一句，新闻把核心事件放在开\n头，无需转折、就是它。\n【替换】keep turning up on the streets ↔ kept turning up\n时态变体的同一短语\nBundles containing thousands of pounds in cash ↔ Bundles of £20 notes\n概括与具体互指同物\n【排除】B)\nB 说 A villager was searching for his lost cash.（一名村民在找他丢失的现金），但录音\n是钱不断\"出现\"、村民是\"捡到\"，从无谁在\"找丢失的钱\"，方向与原文相反。\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 盗用了录音原词 came across 与 policemen 拼成 Local policemen came across\nbundles of £20 notes.，但原文是 Residents of the village have been coming across\nbundles of £20 notes——\"撞见\"现金的是村民居民，不是警察，把发现者张冠李戴了。\n借用原文：Residents of the village have been coming across bundles of £20 notes\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 说 A bundle containing thousands of pounds got stolen.（一捆数千英镑被偷），盗\n用了原文 Bundles containing thousands of pounds 的措辞，却把\"出现并被上交\"歪曲\n成\"被偷\"，与全篇\"现金被发现、还给失主\"的事实相反。\n借用原文：Bundles containing thousands of pounds in cash", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What do the local police do with the money if not claimed in two weeks?", "options": ["A) They give it to charity.", "B) They return it to the finder.", "C) They hand it over to the local government.", "D) They place a notice in The Northern Echo."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They return it to the finder.\n【定位】The owner has two weeks to collect the cash, which is then returned to the finder if not\nclaimed, according to Peterlee Police.\n据彼得利警方称，失主有两周时间来领取现金，如果无人认领，这笔钱随后会归还给发\n现者。\n【信号】条件状语后的处置动词。答案信号是条件 + 处置动词：which is then returned to the\nfinder if not claimed 里 if not claimed（若无人认领）正对题干 not claimed in two\nweeks，紧跟的动词 returned to the finder 就是处置结果。\n【替换】returned to the finder ↔ return it to the finder\n被动转主动的同一动作\n【排除】A)\nA 说 They give it to charity.（捐给慈善），录音通篇没出现 charity 或捐赠的说法，是\n凭\"无主之财常做公益\"的常识硬填，属未提及。\nC)\nC 说 hand it over to the local government（上交当地政府），录音里出现的是 Peterlee\nPolice、Durham Police 这些警方，从未说交给 local government（地方政府），把接收\n对象张冠李戴了。\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 说 place a notice in The Northern Echo（在《北方回声报》登启事），盗用了录音原\n词 The Northern Echo，但那是警察 John Forster 后文受访的报纸，与\"无人认领后怎么处\n置钱\"毫无关系，答非所问。\n借用原文：He told The Northern Echo", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What did the policeman John Forster say about the villagers?", "options": ["A) They cooperated well with the police.", "B) They enjoyed a fairly affluent life.", "C) They were puzzled by the mystery.", "D) They had a strong community spirit."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） They had a strong community spirit.\n【定位】It isn't an affluent part of the world, so for them to find cash of that amount and instantly\nthink about taking it to the police stations shows the community spirit there is.\n这里并不是个富裕的地方，所以他们能捡到那么多现金、还立刻想到把钱送去警局，这显示\n出了当地的社区精神。\n【信号】so…shows 引出结论评价。答案信号是\"因果连词 so + 总结动词 shows\"：so for them to\nfind cash…instantly think about taking it to the police stations shows the\ncommunity spirit，so 后是 Forster 对村民的总结性定性，shows 后面那个词就是他真正\n想说的评价。\n【替换】shows the community spirit there is ↔ They had a strong community spirit.\n结论原词直接对应\ninstantly think about taking it to the police stations ↔ strong community spirit\n行为佐证情感程度\n【排除】A)\nA 说 They cooperated well with the police.（他们与警方配合良好），录音里村民确\n有\"把钱送去警局\"的行为，但 Forster 并未据此评价他们\"配合警方\"，而是上升为社区精神\n——A 是对行为的过度推断。\nB)\nB 说 They enjoyed a fairly affluent life.（过着相当富裕的生活），盗用了录音原词\naffluent，但原文是 It isn't an affluent part of the world——明明说\"不富裕\"，B 把否定\n去掉、反了极性。\n借用原文：It isn't an affluent part of the world\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 说 They were puzzled by the mystery.（他们被这谜团困惑），盗用了录音原词 The\ncircumstances remain a mystery 的 mystery，但感到费解的是警方（Forster 说 remain\na mystery、想 get to the bottom），不是村民，把困惑者张冠李戴了。\n借用原文：The circumstances remain a mystery", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "How does the man often feel on his birthday?", "options": ["A) Excited.", "B) Delighted.", "C) Indifferent.", "D) Strange."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） Strange.\n【定位】I often feel strange on my birthday. It's like my brain decides to have a crisis.\n我过生日时常常觉得怪怪的。就好像我的大脑决定要闹一场危机似的。\n【信号】被问者的直接应答 + 原话情绪词。女士先问\"生日有什么打算\"，男士开口就答 I often feel\nstrange on my birthday，这是被问者的直接应答，且 often feel strange 几乎是直接给出\n心情词，正是答案句的强信号。\n【替换】I often feel strange on my birthday ↔ Strange.\n原词直接对应\n【排除】A) 未提及\nExcited. 是大家默认\"过生日该有\"的情绪，靠常识容易误选，但录音男士从头到尾没说兴\n奋，反而说怪怪的、像要闹危机，与 A 相反。\nB) 未提及\nDelighted.(高兴)同样是想当然的\"生日心情\"，录音并未表达欢喜，男士说的是 strange，故\n排除。\nC) 偷换概念\nIndifferent.(无所谓)看似贴近\"不太在乎生日\"，但录音说的是 feel strange、brain has a\ncrisis，是\"心绪不宁\"而非\"无所谓\"，态度强度对不上。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What does the woman say many people tend to do on their birthday?", "options": ["A) Search for the meaning of their life.", "B) Look back on their years at school.", "C) Call on their relatives and friends.", "D) Talk about future plans with friends."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Search for the meaning of their life.\n【定位】Many begin to search for the meaning of their existence, leading to behaviors such as ending\nor starting a relationship, or plans, like starting a vigorous diet or fitness program.\n许多人开始寻找自己存在的意义，由此引发一些行为，比如结束或开始一段感情，或是制订\n计划，比如开始严格的节食或健身方案。\n【信号】Many begin to… 的群体行为陈述。女士说 Many begin to search for the meaning of\ntheir existence，主语 Many 正对题干的 many people，begin to + 动作 直接给出\"多数\n人会做的事\"，是答案句的明确信号。\n【替换】search for the meaning of their existence ↔ Search for the meaning of their life.\n近义词 existence=life\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音确实提到 their achievements in life in the past decade 里的\"过去十年/过去一年\"，\n让人联想到\"回顾在校岁月\"，但原文说的是回顾\"成就\"、并未提 school 或\"回顾学生时代\"，\nLook back on their years at school. 把\"回顾过往成就\"偷换成了\"回顾学校岁月\"。\n借用原文：their achievements in life in the past decade, or their accomplishments of the past year\nC) 未提及\nCall on their relatives and friends.(拜访亲友)是大家想象中\"过生日\"的常见举动，但录音\n列举的群体行为是\"找人生意义、结束/开始感情、节食健身\"，从未提走访亲友，属常识脑\n补、无原文支撑。\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音里有 or plans, like starting a vigorous diet or fitness program，出现了 plans 一\n词，Talk about future plans with friends. 顺手盗用 plans 设套；但原文的 plans 指\"制订\n节食/健身计划\"这类行动，并非\"和朋友谈未来打算\"，对象与方式都被改了。\n借用原文：or plans, like starting a vigorous diet or fitness program", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What does the man say about birthday celebrations?", "options": ["A) He prefers to have them shown on social media.", "B) He loves them but does not want to make a fuss.", "C) He enjoys celebrating others' birthdays rather than his own.", "D) He looks forward to receiving presents from his close friends."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） He enjoys celebrating others' birthdays rather than his own.\n【定位】I have wonderful friends and I love celebrating their birthdays. But I don't like being the\ncenter of attention, receiving gifts and having a fuss made.\n我有很棒的朋友，我很喜欢为他们庆祝生日。但我不喜欢成为众人焦点、收礼物、被大张旗\n鼓地庆祝。\n【信号】转折 But 切出真意。前半 I love celebrating their birthdays 说爱给别人过，转折词 But I\ndon't like being the center of attention 之后才是落点——爱别人的、却不愿自己被庆\n祝，But 后才是答案信号。\n【替换】I love celebrating their birthdays ↔ He enjoys celebrating others' birthdays\nlove=enjoy、their=others'\nI don't like being the center of attention ↔ rather than his own\n否定句概括成 rather than\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音确有 social media too can intensify things as birthdays now play out more\npublicly，但男士是说社交媒体让生日更公开、加重了他的不安，He prefers to have them\nshown on social media. 却说他\"偏爱在社交媒体上展示\"，与原文态度正相反。\n借用原文：social media too can intensify things as birthdays now play out more publicly\nB) 原词陷阱\nHe loves them but does not want to make a fuss. 用了录音原词 fuss(见 having a fuss\nmade)且\"喜欢但不想张扬\"也部分贴合男士的话，极具迷惑性；但 B 漏掉了关键对比\"喜欢\n的是别人的生日、不喜欢的是自己的\"，泛指 them 反而把\"自己 vs 别人\"这层最核心的区分\n抹掉了，不如 C 精准。\n借用原文：But I don't like being the center of attention, receiving gifts and having a fuss made\nD) 与原文相反\n录音里 receiving gifts 是男士\"不喜欢\"的事，He looks forward to receiving presents\nfrom his close friends. 却把它说成\"盼着收礼物\"，gifts/presents 同义但态度被彻底反\n转。\n借用原文：I don't like being the center of attention, receiving gifts", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What does the woman suggest the man do about his birthday celebration at the end of the conversation?", "options": ["A) Hold it on a modest scale to remove birthday anxieties.", "B) View it as a chance for people to socialize and have fun.", "C) Extend invitation to those he trusts most.", "D) Make it an occasion to collect donations."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） View it as a chance for people to socialize and have fun.\n【定位】Exactly. Or even simply see your birthday as an opportunity to bring people together for them\nto have fun.\n正是如此。或者干脆把你的生日看作一个把大家聚到一起、让他们开心玩乐的机会。\n【信号】结尾处女士的建议句。题干限定 at the end，而这正是对话最后一句女士的话 see your\nbirthday as an opportunity to bring people together for them to have fun，see…as\nan opportunity to 是明显的建议口吻，落在末尾，正是答案信号。\n【替换】bring people together for them to have fun ↔\nView it as a chance for people to socialize and have fun.\n聚人=socialize、机会=chance\n【排除】A) 答非所问\n对话前段女士确实围绕生日焦虑(birthday blues)给过\"别孤立自己、逐步面对\"的话，Hold\nit on a modest scale to remove birthday anxieties. 借\"消除焦虑\"设套；但\"小规模操办\n以消焦虑\"并非女士结尾(at the end)所说，且原文没说\"小规模\"，定位错位。\n借用原文：to deal with the birthday blues, you should not isolate yourself\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音 gradually face your birthday with people you trust 出现了 trust，Extend\ninvitation to those he trusts most. 盗用 trust 设套；但那是女士在对话中段说的，且原文\n是\"和你信任的人一起逐步面对\"，不是\"只邀请最信任的人\"，既非结尾建议、内容也被改\n了。\n借用原文：It's best to gradually face your birthday with people you trust\nD) 张冠李戴\n录音里 asking people to make a donation instead of buying a gift 提到捐款，Make it\nan occasion to collect donations. 盗用 donation；但这是\"男士\"自己提出的想法，题干\n问的是\"女士\"在结尾的建议，张冠李戴了。\n借用原文：asking people to make a donation instead of buying a gift", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What does the woman say about her subway ride?", "options": ["A) It was absolutely exhausting.", "B) There was a terrible smell.", "C) There was too long a delay.", "D) She got off at the wrong station."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It was absolutely exhausting.\n【定位】No, but the train was so packed that I could barely move, and it was difficult to breathe too...\nOnce I did get out, I was totally exhausted.\n不是(晚点)，但车厢太挤了，我几乎动不了，连呼吸都困难……等我终于挤出来，已经累得精\n疲力竭了。\n【信号】先否定再描述，落点在收尾。她先用 No否掉\"晚点\"，再细数挤、喘不上气，最后用 I\nwas totally exhausted收束——\"totally(完全)\"的加重语气正是答案信号，整段抱怨\n归到\"累\"上。\n【替换】I was totally exhausted ↔ It was absolutely exhausting.\n同义词+主被动改写\n【排除】C) 原词陷阱\n男方问 was there a delay?，女方明确回 No。选项 There was too long a delay.正是盗用\n了录音里反复出现的 delay 一词，但\"晚点\"恰恰是她否认的，属听到原词就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：No, but the train was so packed\nB) 未提及\n录音说 it was difficult to breathe too，原因是太挤、不是有味道。There was a terrible\nsmell.借\"呼吸\"联想出\"异味\"，是无中生有的过度引申，原文并未提到任何 smell。\nD) 偷换概念\n录音只说她 could hardly get out(挤得几乎下不了车)，是\"难下车\"不是\"下错站\"。She got\noff at the wrong station.把\"下车困难\"偷换成\"下错站\"，原文无此意。\n借用原文：When I got to my station, I could hardly get out", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "Why hasn't the woman got her own car?", "options": ["A) She hasn't saved enough money.", "B) She is worried about traffic jams.", "C) She hasn't passed the driving test yet.", "D) She is used to taking public transport."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） She hasn't saved enough money.\n【定位】I've got a driver's license, but that's all. I'm saving up to buy something reasonably small and\ncute, but it's still a bit expensive for me, and it'll take a while before I have enough money.\n我有驾照，但也就这样了。我正在攒钱想买辆小巧可爱点的车，但对我来说还是有点贵，得\n过一阵子才能攒够钱。\n【信号】转折 but 后才是真原因。她先说 I've got a driver's license(有驾照)，紧接 but it's still a\nbit expensive for me——转折 but 之后的\"贵、还没攒够钱\"才是真正没车的原因，前半\n句\"有驾照\"是用来排除C的。\n【替换】it'll take a while before I have enough money ↔ She hasn't saved enough money.\n肯否转换+概括\n【排除】C) 与原文相反\n选项 She hasn't passed the driving test yet.借\"driving\"语境制造混淆，但录音开口就是\nI've got a driver's license(已有驾照)，正好与\"还没考过\"相反——没车的卡点在钱，不在\n证。\n借用原文：I've got a driver's license\nB) 张冠李戴\nShe is worried about traffic jams.里的\"拥挤/慢\"其实对应她吐槽地铁公交 The bus takes\ntwice as long and it's just as crowded.，与\"为什么没车\"无关，把别处的抱怨安到这题\n上。\n借用原文：it's just as crowded\nD) 与原文相反\nShe is used to taking public transport.与原文相反：她一开口就在抱怨地铁、还在\nsaving up to buy车，分明想摆脱公共交通，并非\"习惯并满足于\"它。\n借用原文：I'm saving up to buy something", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does the woman say about electric motorbikes?", "options": ["A) They are popular.", "B) They are dangerous.", "C) They are a bit expensive for her.", "D) They are environmentally friendly."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （态度观点题） They are dangerous.\n【定位】I consider that for maybe a minute. But honestly, I've just seen too many horrible accidents\ninvolving those dangerous monsters.\n我也就考虑了大概一分钟。但说实话，我见过太多那些\"危险怪物\"造成的可怕事故了。\n【信号】But honestly 后的强烈贬词。她先敷衍\"考虑了一分钟\"，转折 But honestly后甩出\nhorrible accidents与 dangerous monsters——\"honestly\"加\n重、\"dangerous/horrible\"是强烈负面评价，态度信号集中在此。\n【替换】those dangerous monsters ↔ They are dangerous.\n原词点评直接对应\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nThey are popular.盗用了男方下一句 those popular shared bikes里的 popular，但那是\n评价\"共享单车\"、且出自男方之口，并非女方对电摩的看法，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：those popular shared bikes\nC) 张冠李戴\nThey are a bit expensive for her.盗用了她在买车时说的 it's still a bit expensive for\nme，但\"贵\"评价的是要买的小车，对电动摩托车她说的是危险、从未说贵。\n借用原文：it's still a bit expensive for me\nD) 未提及\nThey are environmentally friendly.听起来很符合\"电动\"的常识联想，但全篇没有任何一句\n说电摩环保，属凭电动二字脑补出来的未提及项。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "How was the woman going to get to work?", "options": ["A) By bus.", "B) By jogging.", "C) By renting a bike.", "D) By sharing a ride."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） By sharing a ride.\n【定位】M: Not if you use a ride sharing app. W: Good idea. I'll download one immediately.\nThank you.\n男：用拼车app就不贵了。女：好主意。我这就下载一个。谢谢。\n【信号】结尾\"Good idea+这就去做\"的拍板。前面公交、单车、慢跑、出租都被她一一否掉或挑\n刺，直到男方提 a ride sharing app，她答 Good idea. I'll download one immediately\n——\"Good idea\"+立刻行动是明确的最终拍板信号。\n【替换】use a ride sharing app ↔ By sharing a ride.\n动名词概括+去掉app\nGood idea. I'll download one immediately ↔ By sharing a ride.\n认可信号坐实最终选择\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nBy bus.盗用对话里讨论过的 bus，但她明确说 The bus takes twice as long and it's just\nas crowded.——又慢又挤，早被否掉，属\"提过即否\"的陷阱。\n借用原文：The bus takes twice as long and it's just as crowded.\nC) 原词陷阱\nBy renting a bike.对应 those popular shared bikes，她回 that's a possibility——只\n是\"一种可能\"，并未像拼车那样\"Good idea+这就去做\"，停在备选阶段。\n借用原文：that's a possibility\nB) 原词陷阱\nBy jogging.盗用她自己提的 jog to work，但她紧接着说 the air pollution is a real\nproblem，因污染把慢跑否了，并非最终选择。\n借用原文：the air pollution is a real problem", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What do we learn about Steve Miller?", "options": ["A) He is a sign language interpreter.", "B) He is a deaf person working in IT.", "C) He doesn't like speaking at meetings.", "D) He doesn't use email or text messages."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） He is a deaf person working in IT.\n【定位】Steve Miller began his career in IT back in the late 1980s. Being a deaf person in tech\nrequired a huge amount of effort in organization.\n史蒂夫·米勒在上世纪80年代末开始了他在 IT 行业的职业生涯。作为科技行业里的一名聋\n人，这需要在组织安排上付出巨大的努力。\n【信号】开篇直接交代身份。篇章开头第一句就点出他的处境——Being a deaf person in tech，介\n绍人物的篇章往往一开口就交代身份，这句即是答案句。\n【替换】a deaf person in tech ↔ a deaf person working in IT\ntech 与 IT 同义、in 改 working in\nbegan his career in IT ↔ working in IT\n'开启IT生涯'='在IT工作'\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n原文确实念到 He needed a sign language interpreter for every meeting，但那是说\n他'需要'一名手语翻译，不是说他'是'手语翻译；选项 He is a sign language interpreter.\n把'他需要的人'安到他自己头上。\n借用原文：He needed a sign language interpreter for every meeting\nC) 偷换概念\n原文说 Communicating in meetings was very difficult.(开会沟通'很困难')，这是客观障\n碍；选项 He doesn't like speaking at meetings. 偷换成'他不喜欢在会上发言'的主观好\n恶，文中从未提他喜不喜欢。\n借用原文：Communicating in meetings was very difficult.\nD) 与原文相反\n原文是 there was no email or text message service——当年(80年代末)根本'没有'这种服\n务，是时代背景；选项 He doesn't use email or text messages. 偷换成'他(现在)不用'电邮\n短信，把'当时不存在'歪曲为'他个人不用'。\n借用原文：there was no email or text message service", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What does Miller say is making things better for people like him?", "options": ["A) Improved communication skills.", "B) Speech recognition technology.", "C) Big advances in sign language.", "D) Transformation in the IT industry."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Speech recognition technology.\n【定位】Big advances in speech recognition technology have enabled communication tools to turn\nlive speech into text in real time on your smartphone or laptop, and sign language into text\nas well.\n语音识别技术的巨大进步，使得沟通工具能够在智能手机或笔记本电脑上实时把现场语音转\n成文字，也能把手语转成文字。\n【信号】因果信号 enabled 点出真正推动者。句子主语 Big advances in speech recognition\ntechnology 后接 have enabled(使……得以)，enabled 是因果信号，前面那个名词正是'带\n来改善'的诱因，即答案句。\n【替换】Big advances in speech recognition technology ↔ Speech recognition technology\n选项是原文核心名词的浓缩\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文反复出现 communication(如 communication tools)，选项 Improved\ncommunication skills. 借这个熟词造干扰，但原文说变好的是'沟通工具'靠技术运转，不\n是'沟通技能'提高了，文中也没说他技能改善。\n借用原文：communication tools\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文确实念到 sign language into text as well，选项 Big advances in sign language. 借\nsign language 造干扰，但原文的'大进步(advances)'是修饰 speech recognition\ntechnology 的，并非'手语本身的大进步'。\n借用原文：sign language into text as well\nD) 过度推断\n原文话题虽在 IT 行业，但带来改善的是一项具体技术；选项 Transformation in the IT\nindustry. 借 IT 一词把'语音识别技术'夸大成'整个 IT 行业的变革'，范围被无限放大，原文没\n说行业整体变革。\n借用原文：a good time to be a deaf person in tech", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "In what way can Miller benefit from attending meetings via video?", "options": ["A) He can avoid being mistaken.", "B) He can take notes on the spot.", "C) He can understand with ease.", "D) He can see the speakers' images."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） He can understand with ease.\n【定位】He can employ lip-reading and have meetings via video instead of on the phone. These\nthings have made a huge difference. He no longer has to worry about whether or not he\ncan understand.\n他可以借助唇读，并通过视频而非电话开会。这些做法带来了巨大的改变。他再也不必担心\n自己能否听懂了。\n【信号】结果句 no longer has to worry 点出好处。在 have meetings via video 之后，原文用\nThese things have made a huge difference 收束，再接 He no longer has to worry\nabout whether or not he can understand——'不再担心能否听懂'正是视频开会带来的好\n处，即答案句。\n【替换】He no longer has to worry about whether or not he can understand ↔\nHe can understand with ease\n'不再担心能否听懂'=肯否转换为'能轻松听懂'\n【排除】A) 未提及\n选项 He can avoid being mistaken. 说'能避免被误解'，原文谈的是他能否'听懂'，从未提\n到'被误解'这回事，属凭空添加。\nB) 未提及\n选项 He can take notes on the spot. 说'能当场记笔记'，原文虽提到技术能把语音转成\ntext(文字)，但从没说他'记笔记'，是把'转文字'歪联想成'记笔记'。\nD) 原词陷阱\n题干和原文都念到 have meetings via video，选项 He can see the speakers' images. 借\nvideo 字面推出'能看到说话人画面'，但原文说视频开会的好处是'不再担心能否听懂'，而\n非'看到画面'本身；越和 video 字面像，越是诱人下钩的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：have meetings via video", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What do people want upon returning home according to the passage?", "options": ["A) To find pure white walls shining.", "B) To enter a house well looked after.", "C) To get a hug from family members.", "D) To see cheerful colours all around."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） To see cheerful colours all around.\n【定位】When people return home, they want to see colours that cheer them up and give them a hug.\n人们回家时，想看到能让自己振奋、给自己一个拥抱的色彩。\n【信号】want 直陈诉求。录音用 they want to see colours that cheer them up 直接点出回家想\n要什么，want to 后面就是答题落点；紧接着 Pure white walls simply don't do that. 又\n用否定把白墙排除，进一步确认想要的是彩色而非白墙。\n【替换】colours that cheer them up ↔ cheerful colours\n动词转形容词\nsee colours ↔ see cheerful colours all around\n动作原词保留\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nTo find pure white walls shining. 盗用了原文里 Pure white walls 这个原词，但原文紧接\n着说 Pure white walls simply don't do that.，白墙恰恰是被否定的、不能给人拥抱的东\n西，听到 white walls 就选正好掉坑。\n借用原文：Pure white walls simply don't do that.\nB) 偷换概念\nTo enter a house well looked after. 借用了原文 They don't look after you. 里的 look\nafter，但原文说的是色彩照顾人（you），不是人想进入一座被照看好的房子，主语和对象\n都被偷换了。\n借用原文：They don't look after you.\nC) 未提及\nTo get a hug from family members. 用了原文的 hug，但原文是 give them a hug——\n给拥抱的是色彩，根本没提 family members，把拥抱的来源安给了原文没说的家人。\n借用原文：give them a hug", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What does the passage say people should avoid doing in home decoration?", "options": ["A) Choosing a colour because it is fashionable.", "B) Painting the interior of their cupboards.", "C) Doing the painting job all by themselves.", "D) Designing all window frames the same way."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Choosing a colour because it is fashionable.\n【定位】Don't go for a colour because it's in fashion, or you've seen it in a magazine.\n不要因为某种颜色正流行、或你在杂志上见过它，就去选它。\n【信号】Don't 否定建议。录音用祈使否定句 Don't go for a colour because it's in fashion 直接\n给出劝阻——别因为时髦而选色，Don't 就是该避免做法的信号词，这句即答案句。\n【替换】because it's in fashion ↔ because it is fashionable\n名词短语转形容词\ngo for a colour ↔ Choosing a colour\n动词同义替换\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nPainting the interior of their cupboards. 盗用了原文 the inside of a cupboard，但原文\n把刷柜子内部当作试色的好办法来推荐，而非要避免的事，态度正好相反。\n借用原文：test your tolerance with smaller spaces, such as the inside of a cupboard\nC) 未提及\nDoing the painting job all by themselves. 里 all by themselves（亲力亲为）原文从未提\n及，录音只谈选什么色、刷哪里，没说该不该自己动手。\nD) 原词陷阱\nDesigning all window frames the same way. 借了原文出现过的 window frame（a\nbright window frame），但原文说的是把窗框作为试色的小空间，没说所有窗框要设计成\n同一款式，更没说要避免。\n借用原文：a bright window frame", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What can one do to make a room look bigger?", "options": ["A) Fit most of the cupboards into walls.", "B) Hang landscape paintings all around.", "C) Match the room's ceiling with all the furniture in colour.", "D) Paint the wooden frameworks and walls the same colour."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Paint the wooden frameworks and walls the same colour.\n【定位】One can also paint the wooden frameworks the same colour as the walls, helping the rooms\nlook bigger.\n也可以把木质框架刷成与墙壁相同的颜色，这有助于让房间显得更大。\n【信号】helping…look bigger 因果。录音里 paint the wooden frameworks the same colour\nas the walls 后面紧跟 helping the rooms look bigger，用 helping 把做法和显大的效果\n直接连起来，正对应题干 look bigger，这句即答案句。\n【替换】paint the wooden frameworks the same colour as the walls ↔\nPaint the wooden frameworks and walls the same colour\n近乎原句改写\nhelping the rooms look bigger ↔ Paint the wooden frameworks and walls the same colour\n效果直接对上题干\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nFit most of the cupboards into walls. 借了原文反复出现的 cupboards，但原文谈柜子是\n说把柜子和墙刷同色（painting the overhead cupboards and the walls in the same\ncolour），没说把柜子嵌进墙里，做法被改造成原文没说的内容。\n借用原文：painting the overhead cupboards and the walls in the same colour\nB) 未提及\nHang landscape paintings all around. 里挂风景画原文完全没说，录音只谈刷颜色，没有\n任何挂画的内容。\nC) 张冠李戴\nMatch the room's ceiling with all the furniture in colour. 借用了原文出现过的 ceiling\n（a painted ceiling）和同色概念，但原文让显大的同色是框架与墙，天花板只是另说能影\n响空间感，没说要与家具同色，把同色的双方张冠李戴。\n借用原文：a painted ceiling is a magical thing without being too obvious", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What does the passage say parents increasingly understand?", "options": ["A) Children must read at least 3 times a week.", "B) Reading is a habit every child can develop.", "C) Reading to their children is important.", "D) Children should start reading at age 3."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Reading to their children is important.\n【定位】Parents often hear that reading to their children is critical for supporting development, and the\nmessage is getting through to them.\n父母常听到『给孩子读书对支持其发展至关重要』这一说法，而且这个信息正在被他们接收\n（理解）。\n【信号】开篇点题句 + getting through 表领悟。答案信号是开篇直接点题：reading to their\nchildren is critical for supporting development，紧跟 the message is getting\nthrough to them，getting through 正对应题干的 understand，是承载答案的那一句。\n【替换】reading to their children is critical for supporting development ↔\nReading to their children is important.\ncritical=important 同义替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 Children must read at least 3 times a week. 把原文 three or more times per\nweek 的频次盗来，但原文说的是『父母给孩子读』的统计数据，不是『孩子自己必须每周\n读3次』的要求，答非所问。\n借用原文：three or more times per week\nB) 未提及\n选项 Reading is a habit every child can develop. 听起来很正确很顺耳，但全文只谈父母\n给孩子读书的好处与选书，从未说『阅读是每个孩子都能养成的习惯』，属凭常识脑补、原\n文无据。\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Children should start reading at age 3. 借用原文 between the ages of three and\nfive 里的 three，但那是统计样本的年龄段，不是『孩子应3岁开始读书』的建议，数字真\n听到了、意思全拧了。\n借用原文：between the ages of three and five", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What can predict children's reading skills according to recent research?", "options": ["A) The number of books they have read by age four.", "B) The speed of their brain development in infancy.", "C) The number and quality of books parents read to them in infancy.", "D) The quality and quantity of time parents spend playing with them."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） The number and quality of books parents read to them in infancy.\n【定位】In fact, recent research has found that both the quality and quantity of shared book reading in\ninfancy predicted later childhood vocabulary and reading skills.\n事实上，最近的研究发现，婴儿期共同读书的质量和数量都预测了孩子日后的词汇和阅读\n能力。\n【信号】研究发现信号 recent research has found + predicted。答案信号是表研究结论的\nrecent research has found 和动词 predicted，predicted 直接对应题干 predict，其前面\n的主语 the quality and quantity of shared book reading in infancy 就是被点名的预测\n因素。\n【替换】the quality and quantity of shared book reading in infancy ↔\nThe number and quality of books parents read to them in infancy.\nquantity=number、shared book reading=books parents read\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 The number of books they have read by age four. 借用原文 four-year-old\nchildren 的 four，但原文 four 是说『四岁孩子身上的发展益处』，从没说『到四岁读了多\n少本书』能预测能力，数字听到了、意思编造了。\n借用原文：four-year-old children\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项 The speed of their brain development in infancy. 借了原文 brain development，\n但原文是说『读书有助于语言和大脑发育』，大脑发育是『结果/益处』，不是『预测阅读\n能力的原因』，把因果位置弄反了。\n借用原文：reading to young children helps their language and brain development\nD) 偷换概念\n选项 The quality and quantity of time parents spend playing with them. 几乎照抄原文\n的 quality and quantity 结构，却把核心动作从『读书』换成 playing（玩），原文谈的始\n终是 reading，不是陪玩，偷换了概念。\n借用原文：the quality and quantity of shared book reading in infancy", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What kind of books are said to help babies learn more?", "options": ["A) Books with specifically labeled images.", "B) Books with pictures of dolls and toys.", "C) Books describing the lives of animals.", "D) Books telling very interesting stories."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Books with specifically labeled images.\n【定位】It followed infants across the second six months of life and found that when parents showed\nbabies books that had faces or objects with specific labels, they learned more.\n研究跟踪了出生后第二个半年的婴儿，发现当父母给婴儿看『带有具体标签的面孔或物体』\n的书时，他们学得更多。\n【信号】因果/条件句 when…they learned more。答案信号是条件结果句 when parents\nshowed babies books that had faces or objects with specific labels, they learned\nmore，learned more 正对应题干 learn more，其条件部分点名的书就是答案；后文 This\nis in contrast to 进一步用对比反衬，强化了这一类。\n【替换】books that had faces or objects with specific labels ↔ Books with specifically labeled images.\nfaces or objects=images、specific labels=specifically labeled\n【排除】B) 过度推断\n选项 Books with pictures of dolls and toys. 把原文 objects（物体）具体化为『玩偶和玩\n具』，原文从未指明是玩偶玩具，是凭常识把抽象的 objects 想象成具体玩具，属脑补。\n借用原文：faces or objects with specific labels\nC) 未提及\n选项 Books describing the lives of animals. 看似贴近『讲动物』，但原文 P3 全程没提\nanimals，更没说『描写动物生活的书』有用，纯属无中生有。\nD) 偷换概念\n选项 Books telling very interesting stories. 把『有用的书』偷换成『很有趣的故事书』，\n原文判定标准是『有没有具体标签』，不是『故事有不有趣』，换了一套评价维度。\n借用原文：books that had faces or objects with specific labels", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What are parents advised to do at the end of the passage?", "options": ["A) Read as many books as possible to their children.", "B) Choose carefully what to read to their children.", "C) Share experience with other parents.", "D) Create picture books for their children."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Choose carefully what to read to their children.\n【定位】Not all books are created equal, so parents need to choose the books they share with their\nchildren with great care.\n并非所有书都一样（有价值），所以父母需要非常用心地挑选要和孩子共读的书。\n【信号】结尾因果建议句 so parents need to…。答案信号是篇章结尾的因果建议句 so parents\nneed to choose the books they share with their children with great care，so 引出结\n论、need to 是直接建议，正是题干要的『结尾建议』。\n【替换】choose the books they share with their children with great care ↔\nChoose carefully what to read to their children.\nwith great care=carefully、share=read to\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n选项 Read as many books as possible to their children. 主张『尽量多读』，但结尾恰恰\n强调 Not all books are created equal『书有好坏之分』、要『用心选』，方向与原文相反\n——原文要的是质而非量。\n借用原文：Not all books are created equal\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Share experience with other parents. 借了一个似曾相识的 share，但原文的 share\n是 the books they share with their children（与孩子共读书），从没说『与其他父母分享\n经验』，对象被换且原文无此建议。\n借用原文：the books they share with their children\nD) 未提及\n选项 Create picture books for their children. 借了原文反复出现的 books 和图像概念，\n但原文建议父母『挑选』书，从没说『为孩子创作绘本』，create 是无中生有的动作。\n借用原文：parents need to choose the books they share with their children", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) additional", "B) associated", "C) composing", "D) cultivate", "E) depressive", "F) directly", "G) emotions", "H) however", "I) implications", "J) interfered", "K) involving", "L) note", "M) precisely", "N) superficial", "O) therefore"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) involving\n动词\n【词性槽】studies 后空、再接 more than 18,000 participants，是分词作后置定语\n【依据】involving = 涉及，studies involving ... participants 是\"涉及……名参与者的研究\"的固\n定说法\nthree studies __(26)__ more than 18,000 participants 填 involving，意为这些研究\"涉\n及/包含\"一万八千多名参与者，学术语境里极常见。\n【竞争词】C) composing 义反：composing 是\"组成\"，主客体方向颠倒\ncompose 指\"部分组成整体\"，应是 participants composing the studies（参与者组成研\n究），而不是 studies composing participants。原文主语是 studies，方向相反，故\ncomposing 语义指向错误，排除。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 C) —— composing 词性形态全对、意思也像\"组成\"，但方向反。\nthree studies __(26)__ more than 18,000 participants——composing 极易误选。防\n坑：代入查主客体方向，studies composing participants（研究组成参与者）不通，应是\nstudies involving participants。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) additional", "B) associated", "C) composing", "D) cultivate", "E) depressive", "F) directly", "G) emotions", "H) however", "I) implications", "J) interfered", "K) involving", "L) note", "M) precisely", "N) superficial", "O) therefore"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) associated\n动词\n【词性槽】was 之后、with 之前的空，是被动语态的过去分词\n【依据】be associated with = 与……相关联，正好连接\"为人父母\"和\"更积极的幸福感结果\"\nparenthood was __(27)__ with more positive wellbeing outcomes for dads 填\nassociated，意为\"为人父母与更积极的幸福结果相关\"，是研究结论里描述相关性的标准\n搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) additional", "B) associated", "C) composing", "D) cultivate", "E) depressive", "F) directly", "G) emotions", "H) however", "I) implications", "J) interfered", "K) involving", "L) note", "M) precisely", "N) superficial", "O) therefore"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) note\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 the authors 后紧跟空、再跟 that 宾语从句，缺谓语动词\n【依据】note that... = 指出、注意到，正好引出研究发现\nthe authors __(28)__ that dads were more likely to take \"playing\" as an __(29)__\nactivity——作者\"指出\"父亲更倾向把陪玩当成一项活动，note that 是论文里转述研究结论\n的常用搭配，语义与时态都吻合。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) additional", "B) associated", "C) composing", "D) cultivate", "E) depressive", "F) directly", "G) emotions", "H) however", "I) implications", "J) interfered", "K) involving", "L) note", "M) precisely", "N) superficial", "O) therefore"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) additional\n形容词\n【词性槽】an 与名词 activity 之间的空，是定语形容词位\n【依据】an additional activity = 一项额外的活动，搭配自然、首音 a 对得上 an\n父亲把\"陪玩\"看作\"额外的/附加的\"一项活动，as an __(29)__ activity 填 additional，语义\n和 an 的用法都成立。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) additional", "B) associated", "C) composing", "D) cultivate", "E) depressive", "F) directly", "G) emotions", "H) however", "I) implications", "J) interfered", "K) involving", "L) note", "M) precisely", "N) superficial", "O) therefore"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) cultivate\n动词\n【词性槽】and 并列 experience，后接宾语 closeness，是动词原形\n【依据】cultivate closeness = 培养亲密感，与 experience positive feelings 并列得当\nexperience positive feelings and __(30)__ closeness with their children 填\ncultivate，\"体验积极情绪并培养与孩子的亲密感\"，cultivate 常与 relationship/closeness\n这类抽象名词搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) additional", "B) associated", "C) composing", "D) cultivate", "E) depressive", "F) directly", "G) emotions", "H) however", "I) implications", "J) interfered", "K) involving", "L) note", "M) precisely", "N) superficial", "O) therefore"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) depressive\n形容词\n【词性槽】fewer 与名词 symptoms 之间的空，是定语形容词位\n【依据】depressive symptoms = 抑郁症状，是心理健康话题的标准搭配\nreporting greater happiness, life satisfaction, and fewer __(31)__ symptoms——父\n亲\"更幸福、更满意、抑郁症状更少\"，fewer depressive symptoms 正好与前面的\nhappiness/satisfaction 形成\"积极更多、消极更少\"的对照，depressive symptoms 是固\n定术语。\n【竞争词】N) superficial 搭配不成立且语境不符：superficial symptoms 非固定术语\nsuperficial 是\"表面的/肤浅的\"，superficial symptoms（表面症状）既非心理健康常用术语，\n也与此处\"幸福/满意/心理症状\"的语境不搭。本句讲心理健康指标，应是 depressive，\nsuperficial 排除。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) additional", "B) associated", "C) composing", "D) cultivate", "E) depressive", "F) directly", "G) emotions", "H) however", "I) implications", "J) interfered", "K) involving", "L) note", "M) precisely", "N) superficial", "O) therefore"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) however\n副词\n【词性槽】两个逗号之间的插入语，是连接副词\n【依据】上文说父亲情况好、本句说妈妈\"不那么积极\"，是转折，用 however\n前一段 Fathers also did better than men without kids, reporting greater happiness 说\n父亲更好，本句 For mums, __(32)__, compared to women without children, the\nresults weren't quite as positive. 转向妈妈\"结果没那么积极\"，前后是对比转折，填\nhowever 最贴。\n【竞争词】O) therefore 逻辑指向反：therefore 表因果，但此处是转折不是结果\ntherefore 意为\"因此\"，要求前句是因、后句是果。但前文讲父亲情况好，与本句妈妈情况差\n之间不是因果，而是对照转折——好坏相反。用 therefore 逻辑不成立，应是 however，故\n排除。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 O) —— therefore 词性对、也是逗号插入位，但把转折看成了因\n果。For mums, __(32)__, compared to women without children, the results weren't\nquite as positive.——很多考生看到插入位的连接副词就随手填 therefore。防坑：先判断\n前后句是\"相反\"还是\"因果\"，父亲好、母亲差是对照，应填 however。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) additional", "B) associated", "C) composing", "D) cultivate", "E) depressive", "F) directly", "G) emotions", "H) however", "I) implications", "J) interfered", "K) involving", "L) note", "M) precisely", "N) superficial", "O) therefore"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) emotions\n名词\n【词性槽】fewer positive 之后的空，是被形容词修饰的名词\n【依据】positive emotions = 积极情绪，与前文 positive feelings 同源\nfewer positive __(33)__ 填 emotions，\"更少的积极情绪\"，与第二段 opportunities to\nexperience positive feelings 的\"积极感受\"一脉相承，且与本句\"妈妈烦恼更多\"对应——\n积极情绪更少。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) additional", "B) associated", "C) composing", "D) cultivate", "E) depressive", "F) directly", "G) emotions", "H) however", "I) implications", "J) interfered", "K) involving", "L) note", "M) precisely", "N) superficial", "O) therefore"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) directly\n副词\n【词性槽】engaging 动词之后的空，修饰动作方式\n【依据】engaging directly in childcare = 直接参与育儿，与前面\"互动时\"对照\nhappier moods while interacting with their kids ... but not while engaging __(34)__ in\nchildcare——妈妈和孩子互动时心情更好，但\"直接参与育儿（杂务）\"时却不然。directly\n强调\"亲自/直接投入\"育儿事务，与\"互动\"形成对照，语义贴切。\n【竞争词】M) precisely 语义不合：precisely 是\"精确地\"，修饰\"参与育儿\"不通\nprecisely 意为\"精确地、恰恰\"，engaging precisely in childcare（精确地参与育儿）讲不通，\n它一般修饰数字/说法的准确度。此处要表达\"直接/亲自投入\"，应是 directly，precisely 排除。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) additional", "B) associated", "C) composing", "D) cultivate", "E) depressive", "F) directly", "G) emotions", "H) however", "I) implications", "J) interfered", "K) involving", "L) note", "M) precisely", "N) superficial", "O) therefore"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) implications\n名词\n【词性槽】important 形容词之后、for 之前的空，是被修饰的名词\n【依据】have important implications for = 对……有重要影响/意义，是固定表达\nmight have important __(35)__ for their wellbeing 填 implications，\"对他们的幸福感有\n重要影响\"，have implications for 是讨论研究意义时的标准搭配。\n【竞争词】G) emotions 搭配不成立：have important emotions for 不通\nemotions 是\"情绪\"，have important emotions for their wellbeing（对幸福感有重要情绪）\n逻辑不通；而且 emotions 更贴 30 空附近的\"积极情绪\"语境。此处讲的是\"对幸福感的影响\"，\n应是 implications，故 emotions 排除。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "People often grant a request just because they want to appear polite and helpful.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】为显得礼貌而答应\n【定位】We say \"yes\" to many requests not because we want to do them, but because we don't want to\nbe seen as rude or unhelpful.\n我们答应许多请求，不是因为想做，而是因为不想被看作无礼或不肯帮忙。\n【改写】they want to appear polite and helpful ↔ we don't want to be seen as rude or unhelpful\n负面词加否定等于正面词\ngrant a request ↔ We say \"yes\" to many requests\n答应请求的同义说法\n【辨邻】 D D段的拒绝顾虑是 The thought of straining the relationship outweighs the\ncommitment of our time and energy——「怕伤关系」，而非 appear polite and\nhelpful 的「怕显得无礼」，动机不同，故不是源头段。\nThe thought of straining the relationship outweighs the commitment of our time and energy", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "It's no easy job learning to say \"no\" to opportunities that were once considered worth grasping.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】对曾值得抓住的机会说 no 不易\n【定位】You still need to say \"no\" to distractions, but you also need to learn to say \"no\" to\nopportunities that were previously good uses of time, so you can make space for better uses\nof time.\n你仍要对干扰说 no，但还得学会对那些「以前是时间好用法」的机会说 no，好为更好的用\n法腾出空间。\n【改写】opportunities that were once considered worth grasping ↔\nopportunities that were previously good uses of time\n曾值得抓=以前是好用法\nIt's no easy job ↔ it can be a tough skill to master\n不容易=难掌握的技能\n【辨邻】 F F段说 You need to say \"no\" to whatever isn't leading you toward your goals——\n拒绝的是「无助于目标的事」，并非 once considered worth grasping 的「曾被认\n为值得抓的好机会」，少了「曾经好」这层转折，故非源头段。\nYou need to say \"no\" to whatever isn't leading you toward your goals", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "When you decline a request, you are saving your future time.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】拒绝即省下未来时间\n【定位】In other words, saying \"no\" saves you time in the future.\n换句话说，说 no 会为你省下未来的时间。\n【改写】When you decline a request ↔ When you say \"no\"\n拒绝请求=说no\nyou are saving your future time ↔ saying \"no\" saves you time in the future\n几乎原样复现\n【辨邻】 F F段说 it retains the most important asset in life: your time——「保住时间这一资\n产」，没有 saving your future time 那种「拒绝即省下未来时间」的明确因果陈述，\n故非源头段。\nit retains the most important asset in life: your time", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "People sometimes struggle to do things that are simply a waste of time.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】费力去做纯属浪费时间的事\n【定位】Even worse, people will occasionally fight to do things that waste time.\n更糟的是，人们有时甚至会争着去做浪费时间的事。\n【改写】struggle to do things ↔ fight to do things\n挣扎做=争着做\nthings that are simply a waste of time ↔ things that waste time\n浪费时间的事，措辞近同\n【辨邻】 I I段说 More effort is wasted doing things that don't matter than is wasted doing\nthings inefficiently——比较两种浪费的多少，并非 struggle to do things that are\nsimply a waste of time 描述的「人主动费劲去做浪费时间事」这一行为，故非源\n头段。\nMore effort is wasted doing things that don't matter than is wasted doing things inefficiently", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Doing efficiently what is not worth doing is the most useless effort.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】高效做不值得做的事最无用\n【定位】I'm reminded of the famous Peter Drucker quote, \"There is nothing so useless as doing\nefficiently that which should not be done at all.\"\n我想起 Peter Drucker 的名言：「没有什么比高效地去做根本不该做的事更无用了。」\n【改写】Doing efficiently what is not worth doing ↔\ndoing efficiently that which should not be done at all\n不值得做=根本不该做\nis the most useless effort ↔ There is nothing so useless as\n「没有比这更无用」=「最无用」", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "It is especially difficult for people to decline to do what their superiors ask them to do.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】对上司之命尤其难拒绝\n【定位】Saying \"no\" to our superiors at work can be particularly difficult.\n对工作中的上司说 no 可能尤其困难。\n【改写】especially difficult ↔ particularly difficult\nespecially=particularly\ndecline to do what their superiors ask them to do ↔ Saying \"no\" to our superiors at work\n拒绝上司之命=对上司说no\n【辨邻】 D D段讲 Collaborating with others is an important element of life 与怕伤关系，对\n象是泛指的合作者，没有 superiors「上司」这一特定对象与「尤其难」的论断，故非\n源头段。\nCollaborating with others is an important element of life", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "People agree to do too many things they are in fact unwilling to do.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】答应太多其实不愿做的事\n【定位】This is not to say you should never attend another meeting, but the truth is that we say \"yes\"\nto too many things we don't actually want to do.\n这不是说你再也别去开会，但事实是，我们对太多自己其实不想做的事说了 yes。\n【改写】agree to do too many things ↔ we say \"yes\" to too many things\n答应做=对…说yes\nthey are in fact unwilling to do ↔ we don't actually want to do\n其实不愿=其实不想\n【辨邻】 B B段说 you're overwhelmed by how much is on your to-do list——答应后被任务\n压垮，讲的是后果；而 unwilling to do「其实不愿做」这一断言出在 A 段，故 B 非源\n头段。\nyou're overwhelmed by how much is on your to-do list", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "According to one famous entrepreneur, innovation means refusal to do an enormous number of things.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】某著名企业家：创新即拒绝大量事\n【定位】Jobs had another great quote about saying \"no\": \"I'm actually as proud of the things we\nhaven't done as the things I have done. Innovation is saying 'no' to 1,000 things.\"\nJobs 还有句关于说 no 的名言：「我对没做的事，和已做的事一样自豪。创新就是对一千件\n事说 no。」\n【改写】innovation means refusal to do an enormous number of things ↔\nInnovation is saying 'no' to 1,000 things\n拒绝大量=对1000件说no\none famous entrepreneur ↔ Steve Jobs\n著名企业家=Steve Jobs", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "It is an essential aspect of life to cooperate with other people.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】与他人合作是生活要素\n【定位】Collaborating with others is an important element of life.\n与他人协作是生活的一个重要要素。\n【改写】to cooperate with other people ↔ Collaborating with others\ncooperate=collaborate\nan essential aspect of life ↔ an important element of life\n本质方面=重要要素", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Refusing a request is sometimes seen as a privilege not enjoyed by ordinary people.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】拒绝被视为普通人无的特权\n【定位】Saying \"no\" is sometimes seen as a luxury that only those in power can afford.\n说 no 有时被看作只有有权者才负担得起的奢侈。\n【改写】a privilege not enjoyed by ordinary people ↔ a luxury that only those in power can afford\n普通人无的特权=只有权势者能享\nRefusing a request ↔ Saying \"no\"\n拒绝请求=说no\n【辨邻】 G G段是 It means saying 'no' to the hundred other good ideas——讲专注就是取\n舍，并非 a privilege not enjoyed by ordinary people 的「说 no 是普通人无的特\n权」这一论点，故非源头段。\nIt means saying 'no' to the hundred other good ideas that there are", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What does the author say is remarkable about the fish we eat?", "options": ["A) They reproduce quickly.", "B) They are mostly farmed.", "C) They have become as important as grain.", "D) They have a longer history than humans."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author say is remarkable about the fish we eat? 用 say 引出作者\n明确表态的内容，不是让考生自行推断。\n【拆句】题干 What does the author say is remarkable about the fish we eat? 主干是 What\ndoes the author say，宾语从句省略了 that，从句里 is remarkable about the fish we\neat 又内嵌一个省略 that 的定语从句 we eat修饰 fish。结构属常规嵌套，无陷阱。\n【定位】For the first time in human history, most of our aquatic(水产的) food now comes from\nfarming rather than fishing.\n人类历史上第一次，我们大多数的水产食物来自养殖而非捕捞。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nThey reproduce quickly 涉及鱼的繁殖能力，原文从第 1 段到第 3 段只谈消费量与来\n源，未出现任何关于鱼繁殖速度的内容。\nB) ✓ 正确\nmost of our aquatic(水产的) food now comes from farming rather than fishing ↔\nThey are mostly farmed\n预判答案是「大多是养殖的」，They are mostly farmed 几乎一一对应——主语 They\n回指题干 the fish we eat，谓语 are mostly farmed 等价于定位句的 most … comes\nfrom farming。\nC) 偷换概念\nThey have become as important as grain 把鱼与谷物相提并论，原文 That grain—\nand the water needed to grow it—could be consumed by people instead 谈的是\n「养鱼比养牛省粮」，并未把鱼的重要性提升到与谷物同等的高度。\nD) 无中生有\nThey have a longer history than humans 称鱼的历史比人类长，文中 For the first\ntime in human history 只是用「人类历史上第一次」这一时间状语，并未对比鱼与人\n类哪个历史更悠久。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What do we need to do if we keep consuming fish at the current rate?", "options": ["A) Increase the fishing volume considerably.", "B) Develop more advanced fishing technology.", "C) Enlarge the marine fish stocks effectively.", "D) Expand the scale of fish-farming continuously."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do we need to do if we keep consuming fish at the current rate? 问的是文\n中给出的具体应对动作，答案应在原文中直接呈现。\n【拆句】题干主句是 What do we need to do，后接 if 条件状语从句 if we keep consuming fish\nat the current rate；keep + 动名词 consuming 表示持续，at the current rate 修饰\nconsuming。结构清晰，对基础薄弱者唯一难点是把 if 从句视作整体条件而非干扰信息。\n【定位】To keep eating fish at the current rate, we're definitely going to need to keep aquaculture(水\n产养殖) developing.\n要想保持目前的吃鱼速度，我们绝对需要让水产养殖持续发展下去。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nIncrease the fishing volume considerably 主张大幅增加捕捞量，而 the volume of\nfish caught in the wild has leveled off since the 1990s 与 more than three-tenths\nare 已经说明海洋鱼资源被过度捕捞，再增加捕捞与原文方向背离。\nB) 无中生有\nDevelop more advanced fishing technology 谈的是发展更先进的捕鱼技术，原文从\nP4 到 P6 完全没有出现 technology、advanced 等任何技术升级方面的表达。\nC) 答非所问\nEnlarge the marine fish stocks effectively 称要扩大海洋鱼类存量，Only a tenth of\nour oceans' fish stocks could sustain heavier fishing than current levels 说明现实\n情况是大多数鱼类资源已被过度捕捞，扩大存量并非作者提出的对策——作者提的对策\n是「转向养殖」。\nD) ✓ 正确\nkeep aquaculture(水产养殖) developing ↔ Expand the scale of fish-farming continuously\n预判要点是「继续 / 发展 / 水产养殖」三个成分；Expand the scale of fish-farming\ncontinuously 用 continuously / expand the scale / fish-farming 三个对应件——同\n义替换全部命中。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What does the author say about China in terms of aquatic food?", "options": ["A) It places increasing emphasis on fishing now.", "B) It boasts of the world's largest fishing stocks.", "C) It raises more fish than caught from the wild.", "D) It supplies 60% of the world's fish products."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author say about China in terms of aquatic food? 围绕中国这个具\n体对象问事实陈述，作者在文中应有正面交代。\n【拆句】题干 What does the author say about China in terms of aquatic food? 主干同 46 题，\n介词短语 in terms of aquatic food 起范围限定，意为「就水产食物而言」。考生若不识 in\nterms of 容易整句失焦，需点明它的限定功能。\n【定位】A lot of that is coming from China, which produces 60% of the world's farmed fish. In fact,\nsome 35 countries, including China, now produce more farmed than wild-caught fish.\n其中很大一部分来自中国，中国生产了全球 60% 的养殖鱼。事实上，包括中国在内的约 35\n个国家如今生产的养殖鱼已经超过野生捕捞的鱼。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nIt places increasing emphasis on fishing now 称中国正在加大对捕捞的重视，但\nfish-farming has been growing at a fast rate. A lot of that is coming from China\n说明中国的重点恰恰是养殖而非捕捞。\nB) 偷换概念\nIt boasts of the world's largest fishing stocks 声称中国拥有世界最大鱼资源量，而原\n文 P6 只说中国生产了全球 60% 的养殖鱼，并没有把 fishing stocks（野生鱼资源储\n量）与中国挂钩。\nC) ✓ 正确\nsome 35 countries, including China, now produce more farmed than wild-caught fish ↔\nIt raises more fish than caught from the wild\n预判指出关于中国的本质判断是「养殖量超过捕捞量」，It raises more fish than\ncaught from the wild 正是这一结构对比；至于 60% 那条数据是关于「全球养殖鱼里\n中国占比」，不是答案。\nD) 偷换概念\nIt supplies 60% of the world's fish products 把原文限定语 farmed fish 改写成 fish\nproducts（鱼制品/水产品总和），范围被悄悄扩大。原文只说中国占全球养殖鱼的六\n成，并未说占全部水产品的六成。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "Why does the author say aquaculture is so important these days?", "options": ["A) It is a must for feeding the world's fast-growing population.", "B) It proves a reliable source of protein for humans and animals", "C) It is essential to maintaining both mental and physical health.", "D) It ensures a balanced healthy diet for people the world over."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 Why does the author say aquaculture is so important these days? 用 say 限定为\n作者明说的原因，不需要考生自行推断。\n【拆句】题干 Why does the author say aquaculture is so important these days? 主句 Why\ndoes the author say 后接宾语从句，从句主干 aquaculture is so important，时间状语\nthese days 表「如今」。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】it's also crucial to ensuring food security and sustainability. By 2050, the world will need to\nfeed an estimated 9.7 billion people.\n（这种向水产养殖的转变）对保障食品安全和可持续性也至关重要。到 2050 年，全世界将\n不得不养活约 97 亿人。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthe world will need to feed an estimated 9.7 billion people ↔\nIt is a must for feeding the world's fast-growing population\ncrucial to ensuring food security and sustainability ↔ a must\n预判核心是「为养活快速增长的人口」，It is a must for feeding the world's fast-\ngrowing population 用 a must / feeding / fast-growing population 三处分别对位\n原文的 crucial / feed / 9.7 billion people——逐一吻合。\nB) 以偏概全\nIt proves a reliable source of protein for humans and animals 称水产养殖为人类与\n动物都提供蛋白质，但 They'll have to get their protein somewhere 中的 They 是指", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What does the author imply by saying aquaculture is no silver bullet?", "options": ["A) Shrimp-farming is a risky business.", "B) Fish-farming will not be sustainable.", "C) Fish-farming may cause serious problems too.", "D) Shrimp-farming can become quite expensive."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （推断题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author imply by saying aquaculture is no silver bullet? 出现\nimply，要求把习语的隐含意翻译成具体含义。\n【拆句】题干 What does the author imply by saying aquaculture is no silver bullet? 主干\nWhat does the author imply，介词短语 by saying aquaculture is no silver bullet 作方\n式状语；by + 动名词短语 saying 后接宾语从句，从句是一句完整的判断句。结构看似复\n杂，实则只需把方式状语整块理解为「通过说这句话」。\n【定位】However, aquaculture is no silver bullet. In some southeast Asian countries, shrimp farming\ndoes disastrous damage to marine ecosystems.\n然而，水产养殖也并非万灵药。在一些东南亚国家，对虾养殖给海洋生态系统造成了灾难性\n的破坏。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nShrimp-farming is a risky business 只锁定对虾养殖一项，而题干问的是 aquaculture\n整体；原文 shrimp farming 只是论据，结论是关于整个 aquaculture 的。\nB) 过度推断\nFish-farming will not be sustainable 把原文「不是万灵药」推断为「将不可持续」，\n跨度过大；Despite these problems, however, shrimp continues to be among the\nmost popular sea foods worldwide 反而暗示养殖仍在继续，并未走向不可持续。\nC) ✓ 正确\naquaculture is no silver bullet ↔ Fish-farming may cause serious problems too\nshrimp farming does disastrous damage to marine ecosystems ↔ serious problems\n预判内容是「养殖也会带来严重问题」，Fish-farming may cause serious problems\ntoo 用 fish-farming 替代 aquaculture、用 may 表示推断的不确定性、用 serious\nproblems 涵盖 disastrous damage——一一对位，且只推一步。\nD) 偷换概念\nShrimp-farming can become quite expensive 把养殖的代价定为「变得昂贵」，原文\ndisastrous damage to marine ecosystems 谈的是对生态系统的破坏，是环境代价而\n不是金钱代价。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the WFP's winning of the Nobel Peace Prize make us realize?", "options": ["A) More and more people in the world are suffering from starvation.", "B) All of us can be affected by food instability one way or another.", "C) It is hazardous to leave millions of people poor and vulnerable.", "D) It is morally wrong to think helping the poor is not our business."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the WFP's winning of the Nobel Peace Prize make us realize? 是用 What\n提问的具体内容题，问的是文中明示的某句话，不涉及推断或主旨。\n【拆句】主干是 What does ... make us realize，the WFP's winning of the Nobel Peace Prize 是\n动名词短语作主语，winning 由所有格 the WFP's 修饰、of 短语补足对象；理解上等价\n于\"世界粮食计划署获诺贝尔和平奖这件事让我们认识到什么\"。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Its Nobel Prize reminds us all of the moral hazard in imagining that the poor and vulnerable\nare somebody else's problem.\n它（世界粮食计划署）获诺贝尔和平奖一事提醒我们所有人：把贫困者与脆弱群体想成是别\n人的问题，是一种道德上的危险。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nMore and more people in the world are suffering from starvation. 说的是饥饿人\n数在增加，这只是 P3 中的一项数据陈述，并非诺贝尔奖让我们意识到的道德层面的\n道理。\nB) 无中生有\nAll of us can be affected by food instability one way or another. 称所有人都会以某\n种方式受食物不稳定影响，原文从未做此论断；P2 强调的是不能把扶贫当别人的事，而\n非\"每个人都会饿肚子\"。\nC) 偷换概念\nIt is hazardous to leave millions of people poor and vulnerable. 说\"让人陷入贫困\n是危险的\"，把\"危险\"指向贫困者的处境；而原文 the moral hazard in imagining that\n... somebody else's problem 中危险指的是\"把扶贫想成别人的事\"这种道德态度，主语\n错位。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthe moral hazard in imagining that the poor and vulnerable are somebody else's problem ↔\nmorally wrong to think helping the poor is not our business\n预判要求的两个关键义素\"道德层面\"与\"不该认为帮穷人与己无关\"都在 It is morally\nwrong to think helping the poor is not our business. 里齐备，匹配最严丝合缝。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What do we learn about the WFP's effort to eliminate hunger?", "options": ["A) It has ensured a sufficient food supply to millions.", "B) It is still far from its goal despite the progress made.", "C) It has done a good job in combating natural disasters.", "D) It is preventing starvation occurring on a global scale."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about ... 问的是关于某一具体话题的明示信息，答案应是原文对该话题\n的直接陈述。\n【拆句】WFP's effort to eliminate hunger 是 about 的宾语；effort 后接动词不定式 to eliminate\nhunger 作后置定语，相当于\"为消除饥饿所做的努力\"。整句无陷阱。\n【定位】Despite decades of effort to eliminate hunger, the latest estimate is that about 11% of people\non the planet (about 820 million people) are suffering daily undernourishment. Progress at\nreducing undernourishment has stopped despite gains through the 1990s and 2000s.\n尽管已为消除饥饿付出了数十年的努力，最新估计仍有约 11%（约 8.2 亿人）正在遭受日\n常营养不良。尽管 20 世纪 90 年代和 21 世纪头十年取得过进展，减少营养不良的进展已经\n停止。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nIt has ensured a sufficient food supply to millions. 称已确保数百万人的充足食物，\n原文 11% of people ... are suffering daily undernourishment 恰恰相反——8.2 亿人\n仍营养不良，谈不上已确保充足供给。\nB) ✓ 正确\nProgress at reducing undernourishment has stopped despite gains through the 1990s and 2000s\n↔ still far from its goal despite the progress made\n预判要求的\"取得过进展\"与\"目标未达\"两要素，分别由 despite the progress made 与\nstill far from its goal 一一承接，且语气与原文同样冷静客观，匹配最优。\nC) 答非所问\nIt has done a good job in combating natural disasters. 把话题偏移到自然灾害，P3\n谈的是消除饥饿、营养不良，未对该机构在自然灾害方面的表现下结论。\nD) 过度推断\nIt is preventing starvation occurring on a global scale. 称正在全球范围内阻止饥饿\n发生，原文 Progress at reducing undernourishment has stopped 明确说进展已停\n滞，更未给出该机构在阻止全球饥饿发生。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What will happen when food aid is offered at a price?", "options": ["A) The rich will become richer and the poor poorer.", "B) More people will be willing to join in the effort.", "C) More food will be made available to the needy.", "D) The relief effort will be rendered less sustainable."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What will happen when food aid is offered at a price? 询问的是某条件下的具体后果，\n文中应有明示的结果句。\n【拆句】主干 What will happen，when 引导时间状语从句 when food aid is offered at a price，\n从句采用被动语态 is offered；at a price 是固定搭配，意为\"有代价地、附带条件地\"，不\n是字面\"以一个价格\"。基础薄弱考生若按字面理解 at a price 易跑偏，需把它当短语整体\n记住。\n【定位】When aid is offered with heavy conditions attached, like loan repayment or food for\nresources, it often widens the gap between rich and poor and sustains the old world order.\n当援助被附加沉重条件（例如要求偿还贷款或以资源换食物）时，它往往会拉大贫富差距并\n维持旧有的世界秩序。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nwidens the gap between rich and poor ↔ The rich will become richer and the poor poorer\n预判中\"贫富差距更大\"的核心义素，恰好被 richer ... poorer 比较级并列对偶句精确锁\n定，且与定位句方向、程度完全一致。\nB) 无中生有\nMore people will be willing to join in the effort. 称会有更多人愿意加入援助行动，\n原文未做此推断，P4 的结果聚焦在贫富差距与旧秩序，与此选项无关。\nC) 与原文相反\nMore food will be made available to the needy. 称会有更多食物送达需要的人，与\nP4 强调附条件援助的负面后果（差距拉大、旧秩序延续）方向相反。\nD) 偷换概念\nThe relief effort will be rendered less sustainable. 把后果转到\"援助行动可持续性降\n低\"，原文未讨论可持续性，只说附条件援助维持旧秩序、扩大差距，未对援助本身能否\n持续下结论。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "How can scientists help cope with poverty and hunger?", "options": ["A) By collaborating closely with world economic institutions.", "B) By sharing expertise with peers in poverty-stricken nations.", "C) By setting up more food research programs in developing countries.", "D) By building self-respect in people suffering from undernutrition."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】How can scientists help cope with poverty and hunger? 以 How 询问途径，文中通常\n以 by 短语或具体动作句给出明示答案。\n【拆句】How can scientists help cope with poverty and hunger? 中 help 后接动词原形 cope\n（省略 to 的动词不定式），相当于\"帮助应对\"；structure 平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】By sharing knowledge of agriculture and climate with peers in countries most vulnerable to\npoverty and hunger, scientists can help reduce these problems.\n通过与最易陷入贫困和饥饿的国家的同行分享农业和气候知识，科学家可以帮助减少这些\n问题。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nBy collaborating closely with world economic institutions. 称与世界经济机构紧密合\n作，P5 提到 world economic institutions 时是否定语气——说该能力不会来自这些机\n构，并未让科学家与之合作。\nB) ✓ 正确\nsharing knowledge of agriculture and climate with peers in countries most vulnerable to poverty\nand hunger\n↔ sharing expertise with peers in poverty-stricken nations\n预判中三个要素\"分享\"\"知识/专业\"\"贫困国家同行\"在 By sharing expertise with peers\nin poverty-stricken nations 中一一对应，结构与原文方式状语完全平行。\nC) 无中生有\nBy setting up more food research programs in developing countries. 称在发展中\n国家设立更多食品研究项目，原文 P5 仅讲分享知识，未提建立研究项目。\nD) 偷换概念\nBy building self-respect in people suffering from undernutrition. 称在营养不良的\n人身上建立自尊，原文 self-confident people using open and shared scientific\nknowledge 谈的是\"自信的人借科学知识自救\"——是结果导向的论断，并非给科学家的\n方法；且 self-respect（自尊）与 self-confident（自信）也并不等同。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What message does the author try to convey at the end of the passage?", "options": ["A) Wealthy nations should solve their own food problems first.", "B) Rich countries should be more generous in providing food aid.", "C) Poor nations should enhance their own ability to solve their food shortages.", "D) World economic institutions should play a bigger role in fighting hunger."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （主旨题）\n【判型】What message does the author try to convey at the end of the passage? 询问作者在\n尾段要传递的核心讯息，需要把末段功能整体串起来，属于局部主旨题。\n【拆句】主干 What message does the author try to convey，at the end of the passage 是地点\n状语限定位置；try to convey 是\"试图传达\"，比单独 convey 更柔和，提示作者意图层面\n的解读。结构清晰，无陷阱。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nWealthy nations should solve their own food problems first. 把 wealthy\ncountries, which may have food problems of their own 这句插入语当作末段主旨；\n插入语只是解释富国为何帮不上忙，并非作者要传达的核心讯息。\nB) 与原文相反\nRich countries should be more generous in providing food aid. 主张富国应更慷慨\n地提供食物援助，而 P5 明确指出能力不会来自富国贷款，P4 更指出附条件援助会扩大\n贫富差距，作者对\"加大富国援助\"持否定态度。\nC) ✓ 正确\nThis ability will be built upon self-confident people using open and shared scientific knowledge\nto pull themselves out of their misery\n↔ Poor nations should enhance their own ability to solve their food shortages\nwill not come from loans from wealthy countries ... or world economic institutions ↔\nPoor nations should enhance their own ability\nPoor nations should enhance their own ability to solve their food shortages. 既覆\n盖末段肯定主线（自身能力解决食物短缺），又自然回应否定主线（不靠富国与机\n构），与末段两层意思全对齐。\nD) 与原文相反\nWorld economic institutions should play a bigger role in fighting hunger. 主张世\n界经济机构发挥更大作用，will not come from ... or world economic institutions 已\n明确把这些机构排除在能力来源之外。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}]});
